  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 place the duty to assess risks and record the significant findings where five or more people are employed</li><li>The big farm killers to assess are transport and vehicles, livestock, falls from height, slurry and gases, and moving machinery</li><li>COSHH covers pesticides and veterinary medicines and needs its own assessment</li><li>A generic assessment printed and filed protects no one; it has to reflect the actual job and be revisited</li><li>The assessment does not control the risk. The control does. The assessment records what could go wrong and what you have done about it</li></ul></div><h2>What a farm risk assessment is actually for</h2><p>The law does not ask a farm to eliminate every risk. It asks the farm to assess the risks that matter and do something sensible about them. On a farm the risks that matter are the ones that kill: farm transport, livestock, falls from height, slurry gases, and moving machinery. A risk assessment does not control the risk. The control does, whether that is fencing the slurry store, handling cattle from a race, or applying Safe Stop. The assessment records what could go wrong and what you have done about it.</p><h2>What the law requires</h2><p>The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 require an employer to make a suitable and sufficient assessment of the risks to workers and anyone else affected, and to record the significant findings where five or more people are employed. The Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 is the general duty behind it. The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations (COSHH) add a specific assessment for pesticides, sheep dip and veterinary medicines. This page is a guide, not legal advice; check the current HSE guidance.</p><h2>The risks worth assessing properly</h2><ul><li><strong>Farm transport</strong> - the single biggest cause of farm deaths. Segregating people from vehicles, safe reversing, and overturning tractors and quad bikes</li><li><strong>Livestock</strong> - being crushed, trampled or attacked, especially cattle around calving and bulls. Handling systems, escape routes and controlling access to stock</li><li><strong>Falls from height</strong> - roofs, trailers, bale stacks and ladders. Edge protection, safe access and fragile roof controls</li><li><strong>Slurry and gases</strong> - drowning in stores and lagoons, and being overcome by hydrogen sulphide on agitation. Ventilation, fencing, and clearing people and animals during agitation</li><li><strong>Moving machinery</strong> - being caught in unguarded parts including PTO shafts. Guarding and Safe Stop</li></ul><p>Children on farms cut across all of these, because the farm is a home as well as a workplace.</p><h2>COSHH on the farm</h2><p>COSHH requires a separate assessment for hazardous substances. On a farm that means pesticides and plant protection products, sheep dip, veterinary medicines, and gases like hydrogen sulphide. The assessment covers safe storage, PPE, application and disposal, and pesticide use also requires the right certificate of competence. The tool records the check and the evidence; it does not decide whether a product is safe to use or replace the label and the certificate of competence.</p><h2>What to look for in a farm risk assessment tool</h2><ul><li><strong>Reflects the real job:</strong> the assessment should capture the actual field and task, not a generic template filed and forgotten</li><li><strong>Voice and photo capture:</strong> a worker can talk the job through and photograph the hazard rather than typing a form</li><li><strong>Revisit and re-record:</strong> easy to redo when the job, stock or weather changes</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes a farm for seasonal and shared labour. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> date, worker, job, hazards and controls, for the HSE, an insurer or an assurance assessor</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the assessment and generates the record. It does not control the risk or decide whether a job is safe to do. That is the competent person's judgement, and pesticide use needs the right certificate of competence. Quickler records what was assessed, with a date and a photo, so the evidence exists. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your farm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a farm risk assessment app?</h3><p>It is a tool that helps a farm assess the risks of a job and record the significant findings and controls. Options range from paper and spreadsheets, to generic audit apps, to conversation-based tools like Quickler that let a worker talk the job through over WhatsApp and photograph the hazard, so the assessment reflects the real work.</p><h3>Does a farm legally have to do risk assessments?</h3><p>The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 require an employer to assess the risks to workers and others and to record the significant findings where five or more people are employed. COSHH adds a duty for hazardous substances such as pesticides and veterinary medicines. There is no single prescribed form. Check the current HSE guidance, as this is a guide and not legal advice.</p><h3>What are the biggest risks to assess on a farm?</h3><p>The risks that kill most often on British farms are farm transport and vehicles, livestock such as cattle, falls from height, slurry and gases like hydrogen sulphide, and moving machinery including PTO shafts. Children on farms cut across all of them. These are the areas where a risk assessment carries the most weight.</p><h3>Can I do a farm risk assessment over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's risk assessment runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The worker talks the job through as a voice note or text, photographs the hazard, and the assessment generates automatically with a date and image. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the farm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-safety-inspection-app-uk.html\">Farm safety inspection app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-machinery-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Farm machinery inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/red-tractor-assurance-records-uk.html\">Red Tractor assurance records</a></li></ul>",
